# A tour of GeoFrame: Europe

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:geoframe, path: Path.join(__DIR__, "..")},
  {:kino, "~> 0.19"},
  {:req, "~> 0.5"}
])
```

## What this notebook does

A walk through GeoFrame on one story, Europe: the countries, its airports, ports, cities and
rivers, then a second region for the coverage tools. Each step uses a different part of the
library (I/O, reprojection, constructive operations, joins, overlay, dissolve, measures) and ends
in a table or a deck.gl map (`GeoFrame.Kino.deck/2`). `notebooks/deck.livemd` covers the map
widget itself and `notebooks/spike.livemd` the first GeoParquet steps, so those are not repeated.

The data is [Natural Earth](https://www.naturalearthdata.com/) vector data (public domain),
downloaded on first run into the system temp directory. Every cell is plain Elixir, and the
cells that use the `filter`, `mutate` or `sort_by` macros start with `require`.

```elixir
base = "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/nvkelso/natural-earth-vector/master/geojson"

fetch = fn name ->
  path = Path.join(System.tmp_dir!(), name)

  unless File.exists?(path) do
    Req.get!("#{base}/#{name}", into: File.stream!(path))
    |> then(& &1.status)
    |> then(&(200 = &1))
  end

  path
end

# Europe as a longitude/latitude box: it drops overseas territories and cuts Russia at 45 E.
europe_box = {-25.0, 34.0, 45.0, 72.0}
```

## Europe, with the Explorer verbs

`read_file!/2` picks the reader from the extension. The result is a `GeoDataFrame`: attributes in
an `Explorer.DataFrame`, geometry in Rust. The Explorer verbs work on it and keep both aligned:
`select`, `filter` and `mutate` (queries, so uppercase column names need `col/1`), `sort_by`, `head`.
Then `GeoFrame.clip/3` cuts the countries to the box, a clip by rectangle. Colour the map by
Natural Earth's sub-region.

```elixir
require GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame

europe =
  fetch.("ne_50m_admin_0_countries.geojson")
  |> GeoFrame.read_file!()
  |> GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.select([
    "NAME",
    "ADM0_A3",
    "SUBREGION",
    "CONTINENT",
    "POP_EST",
    "GDP_MD"
  ])
  |> GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.filter(col("CONTINENT") == "Europe")
  |> GeoFrame.clip(europe_box, keep_geom_type: true)
  |> GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.mutate(POP_M: round(col("POP_EST") / 1_000_000, 1))
  |> GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.sort_by(desc: col("POP_EST"))

GeoFrame.Kino.deck(europe,
  color: {"SUBREGION", :categorical},
  opacity: 0.75,
  tooltip: ["NAME", "SUBREGION", "POP_M"]
)
```

The attributes are an ordinary dataframe (`data/1`), and `pull/2` returns a single column, or the
geometry when given its name; `geom_type/1` then tells what the shapes are.

```elixir
europe
|> GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.pull("geometry")
|> GeoFrame.GeoSeries.geom_type()
|> Explorer.Series.frequencies()
|> IO.inspect()

europe |> GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.head(6) |> GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.data()
```

## Writing and reading every format

The same frame goes to GeoParquet (with the covering bbox column and zstd), GeoPackage, FlatGeobuf,
a zipped Shapefile and GeoJSON, and `write_file/3` chooses the writer from the extension. No GDAL
is needed. The table compares the file sizes.

```elixir
airports =
  fetch.("ne_10m_airports.geojson")
  |> GeoFrame.read_file!()
  |> GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.select(["name", "iata_code", "type", "scalerank"])
  |> GeoFrame.clip(europe_box)

out = Path.join(System.tmp_dir!(), "geoframe_tour")
File.rm_rf!(out)
File.mkdir_p!(out)

targets = [
  {"GeoParquet", "europe.parquet", [write_covering_bbox: true, compression: :zstd]},
  {"GeoPackage", "europe.gpkg", [layer: "countries"]},
  {"FlatGeobuf", "europe.fgb", []},
  {"Shapefile (zip)", "europe.zip", []},
  {"GeoJSON", "europe.geojson", []}
]

for {_format, file, opts} <- targets do
  :ok = GeoFrame.write_file(europe, Path.join(out, file), opts)
end

# A second layer in the same GeoPackage.
:ok = GeoFrame.write_gpkg(airports, Path.join(out, "europe.gpkg"), layer: "airports")

sizes =
  for {format, file, _opts} <- targets do
    {format, file, div(File.stat!(Path.join(out, file)).size, 1024)}
  end

Explorer.DataFrame.new(
  format: Enum.map(sizes, &elem(&1, 0)),
  file: Enum.map(sizes, &elem(&1, 1)),
  kib: Enum.map(sizes, &elem(&1, 2))
)
```

A GeoPackage can hold several layers, and `list_layers/2` and `read_file_info/2` describe a file
without reading all of it. `geoparquet_metadata/1` reads only the Parquet footer.

```elixir
gpkg = Path.join(out, "europe.gpkg")

layers = GeoFrame.list_layers(gpkg)
info = GeoFrame.read_file_info(gpkg, layer: "airports")
meta = GeoFrame.geoparquet_metadata!(Path.join(out, "europe.parquet"))

IO.inspect(Map.take(info, [:driver, :layer, :crs, :geometry_type, :features, :total_bounds]),
  label: "airports layer"
)

IO.inspect(
  Map.take(meta["columns"]["geometry"], ["encoding", "geometry_types", "bbox", "covering"]),
  label: "geoparquet"
)

layers
```

A `bbox:` read returns only the features that touch the box: GeoParquet uses the covering column
and FlatGeobuf its packed R-tree. Both agree with a plain `cx` filter of the full frame.

```elixir
iberia = {-10.0, 36.0, 4.0, 44.0}

from_parquet = GeoFrame.read_parquet!(Path.join(out, "europe.parquet"), bbox: iberia)
from_fgb = GeoFrame.read_flatgeobuf!(Path.join(out, "europe.fgb"), bbox: iberia)
from_cx = GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.cx(europe, -10.0, 36.0, 4.0, 44.0)

names = fn gdf ->
  gdf |> GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.pull("NAME") |> Explorer.Series.to_list() |> Enum.sort()
end

%{
  parquet: names.(from_parquet),
  flatgeobuf: names.(from_fgb),
  same_as_cx: names.(from_parquet) == names.(from_cx) and names.(from_fgb) == names.(from_cx)
}
```

## Reprojection: how much does Web Mercator lie?

`to_crs/2` is pure Rust (`proj4rs`). Area is computed in the CRS units, so measuring in longitude
and latitude is meaningless; use an equal-area CRS or `area_geodesic`. The ETRS89 / LAEA Europe
projection (EPSG:3035) is equal-area, and Web Mercator (EPSG:3857) is not. The ratio between the two
areas is the inflation of each country on a Web Mercator map. `estimate_utm_crs/1` suggests a UTM
zone for local work.

```elixir
require GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame

square_km = fn gdf -> gdf |> GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.area() |> Explorer.Series.divide(1.0e6) end

laea = GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.to_crs(europe, "EPSG:3035")
mercator = GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.to_crs(europe, "EPSG:3857")

geodesic_km2 =
  europe.geometry |> GeoFrame.GeoSeries.area(method: :geodesic) |> Explorer.Series.divide(1.0e6)

inflation =
  europe
  |> GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.put("AREA_GEODESIC", geodesic_km2)
  |> GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.put("AREA_LAEA", square_km.(laea))
  |> GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.put("AREA_MERCATOR", square_km.(mercator))
  |> GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.mutate(MERCATOR_X: round(col("AREA_MERCATOR") / col("AREA_LAEA"), 2))

utm = fn code ->
  inflation
  |> GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.filter(col("ADM0_A3") == ^code)
  |> GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.estimate_utm_crs()
end

IO.inspect(%{Portugal: utm.("PRT"), Poland: utm.("POL"), Greece: utm.("GRC")}, label: "UTM zones")

GeoFrame.Kino.deck(inflation,
  color: {"MERCATOR_X", :viridis},
  opacity: 0.85,
  tooltip: ["NAME", "AREA_LAEA", "AREA_GEODESIC", "AREA_MERCATOR", "MERCATOR_X"]
)
```

The two honest areas agree within a fraction of a percent, while Web Mercator inflates Iceland and
Finland several times over. The largest distortions:

```elixir
require GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame

inflation
|> GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.sort_by(desc: col("MERCATOR_X"))
|> GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.head(8)
|> GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.data()
|> Explorer.DataFrame.select([
  "NAME",
  "AREA_LAEA",
  "AREA_GEODESIC",
  "AREA_MERCATOR",
  "MERCATOR_X"
])
```

## Buffers around airports

Buffers need a metric CRS, so reproject first. The major airports of Europe get a 50 km buffer in
EPSG:3035 (`buffer/3` takes the distance in CRS units, metres here). Then a spatial join finds
the cities of at least 100,000 people inside each buffer, and the largest one becomes the colour of
that airport's circle (on a log scale; circles with no such city stay at the bottom of the ramp). The
circles are small at this zoom, so zoom in.

```elixir
# The bounding box also catches North Africa and the Near East. `dwithin/4` against the union of
# the European countries (10 km of slack for coastal points) keeps what belongs to Europe.
mainland = GeoFrame.GeoSeries.union_all(laea.geometry)

in_europe = fn gdf ->
  gdf = GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.to_crs(gdf, "EPSG:3035")
  near = GeoFrame.GeoSeries.dwithin(GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.geometry(gdf), mainland, 10_000)

  gdf
  |> GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.put("in_europe", near)
  |> GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.filter_with(& &1["in_europe"])
  |> GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.discard("in_europe")
end

cities =
  fetch.("ne_10m_populated_places_simple.geojson")
  |> GeoFrame.read_file!()
  |> GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.select(["name", "adm0name", "adm0cap", "pop_max"])
  |> GeoFrame.clip(europe_box)
  |> in_europe.()

major =
  airports
  |> GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.filter_with(&Explorer.Series.contains(&1["type"], "major"))
  |> in_europe.()

catchments =
  GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.new(
    GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.data(major),
    GeoFrame.GeoSeries.buffer(GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.geometry(major), 50_000)
  )

big_cities =
  GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.filter_with(cities, &Explorer.Series.greater(&1["pop_max"], 100_000))

largest_nearby =
  big_cities
  |> GeoFrame.sjoin(GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.select(catchments, ["iata_code"]), predicate: :within)
  |> GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.data()
  |> Explorer.DataFrame.group_by("iata_code")
  |> Explorer.DataFrame.summarise_with(&[NEARBY_POP: Explorer.Series.max(&1["pop_max"])])

served =
  catchments
  |> GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.join(largest_nearby, on: "iata_code", how: :left)
  |> GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.mutate_with(fn df ->
    [LOG_POP: df["NEARBY_POP"] |> Explorer.Series.fill_missing(1) |> Explorer.Series.log(10)]
  end)

GeoFrame.Kino.deck(served,
  color: {"LOG_POP", :viridis},
  opacity: 0.6,
  tooltip: ["name", "iata_code", "NEARBY_POP"]
)
```

## How much of each country is within 50 km of an airport?

`dissolve/2` without `:by` merges all the circles into one geometry, and `overlay/3` intersects it
with the countries (here in EPSG:3035 so that areas are comparable). Dividing the covered area by the
country's total area gives a share in percent. `how: :difference` returns the rest, the land farther
than 50 km from any major airport. Most countries sit near zero and the city states near 100, so the
map colours the logarithm of the share; hover for the percentage.

```elixir
reach = GeoFrame.dissolve(catchments)

covered =
  laea
  |> GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.select(["ADM0_A3"])
  |> GeoFrame.overlay(GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.select(reach, ["iata_code"]), how: :intersection)

outside = GeoFrame.overlay(laea, reach, how: :difference)

covered_km2 =
  covered
  |> GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.put("COVERED_KM2", square_km.(covered))
  |> GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.data()
  |> Explorer.DataFrame.select(["ADM0_A3", "COVERED_KM2"])

shares =
  inflation
  |> GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.join(covered_km2, on: "ADM0_A3", how: :left)
  |> GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.mutate_with(fn df ->
    covered = Explorer.Series.fill_missing(df["COVERED_KM2"], 0.0)
    share = covered |> Explorer.Series.divide(df["AREA_LAEA"]) |> Explorer.Series.multiply(100)

    [
      AIRPORT_SHARE: Explorer.Series.round(share, 1),
      LOG_SHARE: Explorer.Series.log(Explorer.Series.add(share, 1), 10)
    ]
  end)

IO.puts(
  "Land farther than 50 km from a major airport: #{round(Explorer.Series.sum(square_km.(outside)))} km2"
)

GeoFrame.Kino.deck(shares,
  color: {"LOG_SHARE", :viridis},
  opacity: 0.85,
  tooltip: ["NAME", "AIRPORT_SHARE", "AREA_LAEA"]
)
```

## Nearest port, and rivers within reach

`sjoin_nearest/3` attaches to each city its nearest port; `distance_col:` adds the planar distance,
in metres since both frames are in EPSG:3035. Cities of at least 300,000 people are drawn with the
circle area following the population and the colour following the distance to the sea on a log scale,
so the inland giants (Madrid, Paris, Moscow) stand out.

```elixir
require GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame

ports =
  fetch.("ne_10m_ports.geojson")
  |> GeoFrame.read_file!()
  |> GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.select(["name"])
  |> GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.rename(name: "port")
  |> GeoFrame.clip(europe_box)
  |> in_europe.()

to_port =
  cities
  |> GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.filter(pop_max > 300_000)
  |> GeoFrame.sjoin_nearest(ports, distance_col: "dist_m")
  |> GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.mutate(
    DIST_KM: round(dist_m / 1000, 1),
    LOG_KM: log(dist_m / 1000 + 1, 10)
  )

GeoFrame.Kino.deck(to_port,
  color: {"LOG_KM", :viridis},
  radius: "pop_max",
  opacity: 0.8,
  tooltip: ["name", "adm0name", "pop_max", "port", "DIST_KM"]
)
```

The farthest from the coast:

```elixir
require GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame

to_port
|> GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.sort_by(desc: col("DIST_KM"))
|> GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.head(8)
|> GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.data()
|> Explorer.DataFrame.select(["name", "adm0name", "pop_max", "port", "DIST_KM"])
```

Rivers are lines, and `sjoin/3` accepts the `:dwithin` predicate with a distance. Which cities lie within
10 km of a Natural Earth river centerline? A left join keeps the cities that
touch no river; one that touches several rivers appears once per river, so `distinct/2` keeps one row
per city. To draw the rivers and the two kinds of city on the same map, stack the frames with
`concat/1` after giving each a `kind` column.

```elixir
require GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame

rivers =
  fetch.("ne_50m_rivers_lake_centerlines.geojson")
  |> GeoFrame.read_file!()
  |> GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.select(["name"])
  |> GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.drop_nil()
  |> GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.to_crs("EPSG:3035")
  |> GeoFrame.clip(mainland)

towns = GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.filter(cities, pop_max > 100_000)

marked =
  towns
  |> GeoFrame.sjoin(GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.rename(rivers, name: "river"),
    how: :left,
    predicate: :dwithin,
    distance: 10_000
  )
  |> GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.distinct(["name", "pop_max"])
  |> GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.mutate_with(fn df ->
    [kind: Explorer.Series.select(Explorer.Series.is_nil(df["river"]), "city", "city on a river")]
  end)

rivers_kind =
  rivers
  |> GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.put(
    "kind",
    List.duplicate("river", GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.n_rows(rivers))
  )

stacked =
  GeoFrame.concat([
    GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.select(rivers_kind, ["name", "kind"]),
    GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.select(marked, ["name", "kind"])
  ])

on_river = GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.filter(marked, kind == "city on a river")

IO.puts(
  "#{GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.n_rows(on_river)} of #{GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.n_rows(towns)} cities are within 10 km of a river"
)

GeoFrame.Kino.deck(stacked,
  color: {"kind", :categorical},
  radius: 4,
  width: 1.5,
  opacity: 0.85,
  tooltip: ["name", "kind"]
)
```

## Simplifying and measuring the error

`simplify/3` with `preserve_topology: false` is Ramer-Douglas-Peucker, where the tolerance is a
distance in CRS units. How wrong is the result? `hausdorff_distance/3` measures, row by row, the
largest gap between two geometries. The table shows vertex counts and the mean and worst error at
three tolerances; the map draws the 20 km version and colours each country by the share of its vertices that
were dropped (ragged coasts lose the most, straight borders the least).

```elixir
original = laea.geometry

variant = fn tolerance ->
  GeoFrame.GeoSeries.simplify(original, tolerance, preserve_topology: false)
end

vertices = fn series ->
  series |> GeoFrame.GeoSeries.count_coordinates() |> Explorer.Series.sum()
end

rows =
  for km <- [2, 5, 20] do
    simple = variant.(km * 1000)
    error = GeoFrame.GeoSeries.hausdorff_distance(simple, original)

    %{
      tolerance_km: km,
      vertices: vertices.(simple),
      mean_error_km: error |> Explorer.Series.mean() |> Kernel./(1000) |> Float.round(1),
      worst_error_km: error |> Explorer.Series.max() |> Kernel./(1000) |> Float.round(1)
    }
  end

IO.inspect(vertices.(original), label: "original vertices")

rows
|> Explorer.DataFrame.new()
|> Explorer.DataFrame.select(["tolerance_km", "vertices", "mean_error_km", "worst_error_km"])
```

```elixir
simple20 = variant.(20_000)

removed =
  Explorer.Series.subtract(
    1,
    Explorer.Series.divide(
      GeoFrame.GeoSeries.count_coordinates(simple20),
      GeoFrame.GeoSeries.count_coordinates(original)
    )
  )
  |> Explorer.Series.multiply(100)
  |> Explorer.Series.round(0)

error_km =
  simple20
  |> GeoFrame.GeoSeries.hausdorff_distance(original)
  |> Explorer.Series.divide(1000)

drawn =
  GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.new(
    laea |> GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.data() |> Explorer.DataFrame.select(["NAME", "POP_M"]),
    simple20
  )
  |> GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.put("VERTICES_REMOVED_PCT", removed)
  |> GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.put("ERROR_KM", Explorer.Series.round(error_km, 1))

GeoFrame.Kino.deck(drawn,
  color: {"VERTICES_REMOVED_PCT", :viridis},
  opacity: 0.85,
  tooltip: ["NAME", "VERTICES_REMOVED_PCT", "ERROR_KM"]
)
```

Lines have the discrete Fréchet distance too (`frechet_distance/3`, for `LineString` rows; the `:densify`
fraction adds points along the segments so that it is not measured at the vertices only). The same
check on the rivers, simplified to 5 km:

```elixir
lines =
  rivers |> GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.explode(ignore_index: true) |> GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.geometry()

simple_lines = GeoFrame.GeoSeries.simplify(lines, 5_000, preserve_topology: false)
leash = GeoFrame.GeoSeries.frechet_distance(simple_lines, lines, densify: 0.02)

%{
  river_parts: GeoFrame.GeoSeries.size(lines),
  vertices_before: vertices.(lines),
  vertices_after: vertices.(simple_lines),
  worst_frechet_km: leash |> Explorer.Series.max() |> Kernel./(1000) |> Float.round(1)
}
```

## How convex is a country?

`convex_hull/1` wraps each geometry in the smallest convex polygon. The area of the country divided by
the area of its hull is its solidity: 1 for a convex shape, lower for coastlines with gulfs, islands and
peninsulas. The centroid of such shapes can fall outside the shape, which is what `representative_point/1`
fixes (the point is guaranteed to lie inside).

```elixir
require GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame

hull = GeoFrame.GeoSeries.convex_hull(laea.geometry)

solidity =
  Explorer.Series.divide(GeoFrame.GeoSeries.area(laea.geometry), GeoFrame.GeoSeries.area(hull))

shapes =
  laea
  |> GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.select(["NAME", "SUBREGION"])
  |> GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.put("SOLIDITY", Explorer.Series.round(solidity, 2))

centre_inside =
  GeoFrame.GeoSeries.within(GeoFrame.GeoSeries.centroid(laea.geometry), laea.geometry)

outside_names =
  shapes
  |> GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.put("centroid_inside", centre_inside)
  |> GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.filter(centroid_inside == false)
  |> GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.pull("NAME")
  |> Explorer.Series.to_list()

IO.inspect(outside_names, label: "centroid falls outside the country")

representative_inside =
  laea.geometry
  |> GeoFrame.GeoSeries.representative_point()
  |> GeoFrame.GeoSeries.within(laea.geometry)
  |> Explorer.Series.to_list()
  |> Enum.all?()

IO.inspect(representative_inside, label: "representative point always inside")

GeoFrame.Kino.deck(shapes,
  color: {"SOLIDITY", :viridis},
  opacity: 0.85,
  tooltip: ["NAME", "SOLIDITY"]
)
```

## Dissolve countries into regions

`dissolve/2` merges the rows of each group into one geometry (`union_all` per group, in Rust) and
aggregates the attributes with Explorer: here the population and the GDP are summed and the countries
counted. Natural Earth gives GDP in millions of US dollars, so the GDP per head is a ratio of two sums.

```elixir
require GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame

regions =
  europe
  |> GeoFrame.dissolve(
    by: "SUBREGION",
    aggfunc: [POP_EST: :sum, GDP_MD: :sum, NAME: :count]
  )
  |> GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.mutate(
    GDP_PER_HEAD: round(col("GDP_MD") * 1_000_000 / col("POP_EST"), 0),
    POP_M: round(col("POP_EST") / 1_000_000, 1)
  )

GeoFrame.Kino.deck(regions,
  color: {"GDP_PER_HEAD", :viridis},
  opacity: 0.8,
  tooltip: ["SUBREGION", "NAME", "POP_M", "GDP_PER_HEAD"]
)
```

## Explode into polygons

A country is often a `MultiPolygon`: the mainland plus islands. `explode/2` makes one row per part and
keeps the attributes (`index_parts: true` adds the `index` of the source row and the `part` number).
Colouring the parts by the logarithm of their area separates the mainlands from the islands.

```elixir
require GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame

parts =
  laea
  |> GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.select(["NAME"])
  |> GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.explode(index_parts: true)

parts =
  parts
  |> GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.put("AREA_KM2", parts |> square_km.() |> Explorer.Series.round(1))
  |> GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.mutate(LOG_AREA: log(col("AREA_KM2") + 1, 10))

IO.puts(
  "#{GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.n_rows(laea)} countries, #{GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.n_rows(parts)} polygons"
)

GeoFrame.Kino.deck(parts,
  color: {"LOG_AREA", :viridis},
  opacity: 0.9,
  tooltip: ["NAME", "part", "AREA_KM2"]
)
```

The countries made of the most pieces, using `group_by` on the attribute side:

```elixir
require Explorer.DataFrame

parts
|> GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.data()
|> Explorer.DataFrame.group_by("NAME")
|> Explorer.DataFrame.summarise_with(
  &[PARTS: Explorer.Series.count(&1["part"]), KM2: Explorer.Series.sum(&1["AREA_KM2"])]
)
|> Explorer.DataFrame.sort_by(desc: col("PARTS"))
|> Explorer.DataFrame.head(8)
```

## Distances between capitals

`distance/3` pairs rows of two series, or compares each row with one geometry, and `method: :geodesic`
returns metres on the WGS 84 ellipsoid (the points are back in longitude/latitude for it). Looping over the capitals
builds the distance matrix, one column per city.

```elixir
require GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame

wanted = ["Lisbon", "Madrid", "Paris", "Berlin", "Rome", "Warsaw", "Athens", "Stockholm"]

capitals =
  cities
  |> GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.to_crs("EPSG:4326")
  |> GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.filter(adm0cap == 1)
  |> GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.filter_with(&Explorer.Series.in(&1["name"], wanted))

capital_names = capitals |> GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.pull("name") |> Explorer.Series.to_list()

columns =
  for {name, row} <- Enum.with_index(capital_names) do
    one = GeoFrame.GeoSeries.take(capitals.geometry, [row])

    km =
      capitals.geometry
      |> GeoFrame.GeoSeries.distance(one, method: :geodesic)
      |> Explorer.Series.divide(1000)
      |> Explorer.Series.round(0)

    {name, km}
  end

Explorer.DataFrame.new([{"from", Explorer.Series.from_list(capital_names)} | columns])
```

The matrix is symmetric with a zero diagonal. Lisbon to Athens is about 2,860 km on the ellipsoid.

## Coverage tools on the states of the USA

Administrative areas should tile the plane with no gaps or overlaps, and simplifying them one by one
breaks that. `is_valid_coverage/2` checks it, `invalid_coverage_edges/2` returns the offending edges and
`simplify_coverage/3` simplifies the shared borders once so that neighbours still fit. The 48 contiguous
states from the admin-1 file, in the equal-area Albers CRS (EPSG:5070), are simplified with a
25 km tolerance in both ways.

```elixir
require GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame

states =
  fetch.("ne_50m_admin_1_states_provinces.geojson")
  |> GeoFrame.read_file!()
  |> GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.select(["name", "postal", "region", "admin"])
  |> GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.filter(admin == "United States of America")
  |> GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.filter(postal != "AK" and postal != "HI")
  |> GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.to_crs("EPSG:5070")

original = states.geometry
alone = GeoFrame.GeoSeries.simplify(original, 25_000, preserve_topology: false)
together = GeoFrame.GeoSeries.simplify_coverage(original, 25_000)

count = fn series -> series |> GeoFrame.GeoSeries.count_coordinates() |> Explorer.Series.sum() end

Explorer.DataFrame.new(
  version: ["original", "simplify, state by state", "simplify_coverage"],
  vertices: [count.(original), count.(alone), count.(together)],
  valid_coverage: [
    GeoFrame.GeoSeries.is_valid_coverage(original),
    GeoFrame.GeoSeries.is_valid_coverage(alone),
    GeoFrame.GeoSeries.is_valid_coverage(together)
  ]
)
```

Where do the independent results disagree? Each state gets the stretches of its border that overlap a
neighbour or fail to match its vertices. Those edges are the seams that `simplify/3` opened. The map
stacks the valid `simplify_coverage` states (blue) with the seams of the independent result (orange), told
apart by a `kind` column.

```elixir
require GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame

seams = GeoFrame.GeoSeries.invalid_coverage_edges(alone)

kind = fn gdf, label ->
  gdf
  |> GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.select(["name"])
  |> GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.put("kind", List.duplicate(label, GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.n_rows(gdf)))
end

tidy =
  states
  |> GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.data()
  |> GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.new(together)

broken =
  states
  |> GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.data()
  |> GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.new(seams)
  |> GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.put("clean", GeoFrame.GeoSeries.is_empty(seams))
  |> GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.filter(clean == false)

GeoFrame.Kino.deck(
  GeoFrame.concat([
    kind.(tidy, "state, simplify_coverage"),
    kind.(broken, "seam, state by state")
  ]),
  color: {"kind", :categorical},
  width: 3,
  opacity: 0.7,
  tooltip: ["name", "kind"]
)
```
